1
00:00:18,172 --> 00:00:19,333
Good evening, everyone.

2
00:00:19,353 --> 00:00:21,657
I trust you've had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:21,876 --> 00:00:22,937
I do not say happy.

4
00:00:22,957 --> 00:00:24,500
I hope it has been happy.

5
00:00:24,539 --> 00:00:33,192
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sambha sambhodasa.

6
00:00:33,912 --> 00:00:41,142
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sambha sambhodasa.

7
00:00:41,645 --> 00:01:05,340
homage to the buddha the blessed noble and fully self-awakened so i thought this evening we'd have a go at the diganaka sutta the discourse to diganakara on dikanaka

8
00:01:06,754 --> 00:01:10,540
So here we're back at the Boar's Cave on the Vulture's Peak.

9
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:22,515
Vulture's Peak, remember, is near Rajgir on the mountains that surround the capital of Rajgir, modern-day Rajgir, and it's called Rajagiri, the town of the kings.

10
00:01:22,575 --> 00:01:31,445
That's where Bimbisara lived and ruled over Magadha, which is on the banks of the Ganges.

11
00:01:32,724 --> 00:02:00,808
so uh having uh done the usual thing of courteous and annual talk uh diganaka says by the way diganaka is the nephew of sariputta so remember sariputta is the um one of the two two chief disciples of the buddha the other one is magalana and sariputta was known for his understanding of the dharma second only to the buddha here he's actually just joined the order right

12
00:02:01,581 --> 00:02:03,984
So we'll come to him at the end.

13
00:02:05,146 --> 00:02:12,813
So now Dighanaka approaches the Buddha and he says, my doctrine of view, this is what I believe, is nothing is acceptable to me.

14
00:02:13,014 --> 00:02:15,477
In other words, I don't believe in anything.

15
00:02:15,497 --> 00:02:21,203
So the Buddha replies, well, you believe in this anyway, that this is the correct view.

16
00:02:21,263 --> 00:02:22,343
Nothing is acceptable.

17
00:02:23,465 --> 00:02:29,951
And he says, well, he says, it doesn't matter whether I believe it or not.

18
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:35,324
Now, these people were called eel wrigglers.

19
00:02:36,947 --> 00:02:40,450
So there's a passage here in another discourse.

20
00:02:40,471 --> 00:02:43,215
A certain teacher is dull and confused.

21
00:02:43,836 --> 00:02:50,864
Because he's dull and confused, when he's asked such and such a question, he engages in verbal wriggling, in eel wriggling.

22
00:02:51,905 --> 00:02:53,347
I don't say it is like this.

23
00:02:53,627 --> 00:02:54,949
I don't say it is like that.

24
00:02:55,669 --> 00:02:56,890
I don't say it is otherwise.

25
00:02:57,331 --> 00:02:59,313
And I don't say it is not so.

26
00:02:59,530 --> 00:03:01,532
And I don't say it is not not so.

27
00:03:02,354 --> 00:03:06,942
So these are the people who are real skeptics, who don't come down either one way or the other.

28
00:03:08,685 --> 00:03:19,223
Now, the Buddha replies, well, there are some people who say nothing, you know, I don't believe in anything.

29
00:03:19,663 --> 00:03:24,170
But then actually, when you look underneath it all, they do actually believe in something.

30
00:03:24,674 --> 00:03:30,501
And some people who say, well, I believe in everything, when you look underneath it, they don't really have a belief.

31
00:03:30,842 --> 00:03:40,252
So in other words, it's being a skeptic, neither this nor that nor nothing else, is a difficult position to hold.

32
00:03:40,734 --> 00:03:43,576
As human beings, we don't like to be in a state of don't know.

33
00:03:43,597 --> 00:03:45,079
We like to be certain.

34
00:03:46,611 --> 00:03:49,474
So now there are these two opposing doctrines.

35
00:03:49,555 --> 00:03:56,783
The one is about eternalism, that after death, the soul goes on, the personality, et cetera, goes on and on.

36
00:03:57,123 --> 00:04:02,730
And the other one is annihilation that came to modern day scientific materialists.

37
00:04:04,233 --> 00:04:08,016
So now the Buddha says something interesting here.

38
00:04:08,056 --> 00:04:16,466
He says, when those people say everything is acceptable to me, in other words, I'm an eternalist,

39
00:04:17,257 --> 00:04:25,908
They are close to indulgence, close to bondage, close to holding and clinging, right?

40
00:04:25,928 --> 00:04:32,317
So underneath all eternalism is this desire to keep becoming.

41
00:04:32,336 --> 00:04:35,161
In the view of those Brahmins as a whole, nothing is acceptable.

42
00:04:35,221 --> 00:04:42,891
They are close to non-indulgence, non-bondage, close to non-holding, non-clinging, right?

43
00:04:42,911 --> 00:04:43,952
Because what's the point?

44
00:04:44,394 --> 00:04:45,475
Are you going to die anyway?

45
00:04:46,012 --> 00:04:57,889
Now, Dighanaga jumps on this and says, ah, Master Gautama commends my view then, because he knows the Buddha's teaching about letting go, relinquishment, not self.

46
00:04:57,908 --> 00:05:02,815
And he says, well, he then goes on.

47
00:05:03,055 --> 00:05:10,586
He sort of ignores that and says, those people who stand in between, something is acceptable and something is not acceptable.

48
00:05:10,607 --> 00:05:12,930
I believe certain things, but I don't believe in others.

49
00:05:13,247 --> 00:05:15,050
They suffer from both sides.

50
00:05:15,771 --> 00:05:19,815
Where they believe in something, it's a holding on, a desire to become.

51
00:05:19,956 --> 00:05:25,423
Where they don't believe in something, then it's just another way of annihilationism.

52
00:05:29,649 --> 00:05:34,014
Now we come to a session where the Buddha points out what the danger is here.

53
00:05:35,096 --> 00:05:43,086
Everything is, I believe in everything, is if I obstinately adhere to my view, everything is acceptable to me.

54
00:05:43,437 --> 00:06:02,333
only this is true, everything else is wrong, then I may clash with the other two, with the Brahmin who says, or with the recluse or Brahmin who says, nothing is acceptable, and with the one who says, something is acceptable, but something is not acceptable to me.

55
00:06:03,254 --> 00:06:07,737
Now, I may clash with these two, and when there's a clash, there's disputes.

56
00:06:08,257 --> 00:06:10,180
When there are disputes, there are quarrels.

57
00:06:10,300 --> 00:06:12,482
When there are quarrels, there's vexation.

58
00:06:13,221 --> 00:06:23,230
For seeing for himself clashes, disputes, quarrels and vexation, he abandons that view and does not take up some other view.

59
00:06:24,151 --> 00:06:27,875
This is how there comes to be an abandoning of these views.

60
00:06:28,396 --> 00:06:32,059
This is how there comes to be a relinquishing of these views.

61
00:06:32,180 --> 00:06:35,141
Now, what does the Buddha mean by views?

62
00:06:35,281 --> 00:06:38,625
Because opinions, because, you know, we have to have opinions.

63
00:06:38,646 --> 00:06:41,288
We have to have views.

64
00:06:41,858 --> 00:06:51,730
Every time we vote for a particular government, every time we vote for a particular party, we're coming from a particular view, a particular opinion.

65
00:06:53,012 --> 00:06:58,658
But he's making a distinction here between a view, an opinion and a perspective.

66
00:06:59,278 --> 00:07:01,562
So a view has three parts to it.

67
00:07:02,043 --> 00:07:03,985
The view is wrong or it's partial.

68
00:07:05,887 --> 00:07:07,870
It has this sense of conceit.

69
00:07:08,170 --> 00:07:10,312
I'm right and all these other people are silly.

70
00:07:10,882 --> 00:07:21,577
and it has an emotional commitment, and that's the tanha, mine, which is my view, and that's where the clashing and the anger and all that comes from.

71
00:07:22,338 --> 00:07:40,283
Whereas if you have a perspective, if you see your view and opinion as a perspective, you're actually open to everybody else's view and perspective, some of which, of course, you might not agree with, but because there's not that holding on, I'm right and you're wrong, then there's no disputes.

72
00:07:40,685 --> 00:07:45,271
So he says the same thing of people who say nothing is acceptable to me.

73
00:07:46,012 --> 00:07:53,766
They will clash with people who say everything is acceptable and with those who say some things are and some things are not acceptable.

74
00:07:53,786 --> 00:07:58,293
And the same with those who say something is acceptable, something is not acceptable.

75
00:07:58,673 --> 00:08:00,216
I believe in this and not in that.

76
00:08:00,615 --> 00:08:04,903
They will clash with people who say this is right and that is wrong.

77
00:08:07,600 --> 00:08:10,545
So the Buddha says, leave all this, right?

78
00:08:10,745 --> 00:08:21,081
Forget this thinking, thinking, thinking, thinking, views and opinions, and bring yourself down to the real investigation of the way things are.

79
00:08:21,122 --> 00:08:24,387
So he begins to teach him how to do the Vipassana.

80
00:08:24,406 --> 00:08:30,117
So the first thing is the body, made of material form consisting of the four great elements.

81
00:08:30,257 --> 00:08:32,780
So if you remember those, fire, earth, water and air.

82
00:08:33,537 --> 00:08:45,751
procreated by mother and father and built up out of boiled rice and porridge, is subject to impermanence, to being worn and rubbed away, to dissolution and disintegration.

83
00:08:46,613 --> 00:09:01,451
It should be regarded as impermanent, as suffering, a disease, a tumour, a dart, a calamity, an affliction, as alien, as disintegrating, as void and as not self.

84
00:09:03,355 --> 00:09:12,184
When one regards the body, thus one abandons desire for the body, affection for the body, attachment to the body and subservience to the body.

85
00:09:12,245 --> 00:09:15,669
In other words, being controlled by bodily desires.

86
00:09:17,751 --> 00:09:28,705
So having taught him how to look at the body, which remember is the first of the four establishments of mindfulness or awareness in the Satipatthana discourse, the discourse on how to establish it.

87
00:09:29,072 --> 00:09:31,296
First section, if you remember, is on the body.

88
00:09:31,836 --> 00:09:33,458
The second section is on feeling.

89
00:09:34,400 --> 00:09:36,844
So he asked him to investigate feeling.

90
00:09:36,884 --> 00:09:42,152
And he says, look, there are three types of feeling, pleasant, unpleasant and neutral.

91
00:09:44,215 --> 00:09:49,701
When you feel a pleasant feeling, you'll notice that you cannot feel an unpleasant feeling.

92
00:09:49,721 --> 00:09:51,865
You can't feel a neutral feeling.

93
00:09:52,105 --> 00:09:53,567
And so it is for the other three.

94
00:09:54,269 --> 00:09:57,173
One of them always knocks out the other two.

95
00:09:59,025 --> 00:10:03,892
And then you'll notice that they're impermanent, they're conditioned, dependently arisen.

96
00:10:04,192 --> 00:10:11,000
By the way, sorry, the reason he's pointed out that there are three types and the one cannot appear with the other is because of identity.

97
00:10:11,480 --> 00:10:12,140
I'm happy.

98
00:10:12,322 --> 00:10:13,783
Next minute, I'm sad.

99
00:10:13,822 --> 00:10:14,904
So which one is the I?

100
00:10:15,044 --> 00:10:16,225
Which one is the true you?

101
00:10:18,128 --> 00:10:23,835
Pleasant feeling, Dighanaka, is impermanent, conditioned.

102
00:10:24,153 --> 00:10:29,841
Dependently arisen, subject to destruction, vanishing, fading away and ceasing.

103
00:10:30,322 --> 00:10:36,091
And that's true, of course, of painful and neutral feelings.

104
00:10:36,111 --> 00:10:48,270
So now he stops here and he says, a well-taught noble disciple becomes disenchanted with pleasant feelings, disenchanted with painful feelings, disenchanted with neutral feelings.

105
00:10:49,032 --> 00:10:51,696
And being disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate.

106
00:10:52,267 --> 00:11:15,101
through dispassion is liberated when he's liberated there comes the knowledge it is liberated he understands birth is destroyed the holy life is lived what had to be done has been done and there's no more for me coming into a state of becoming now the bhikkhu whose mind is liberated thus

107
00:11:15,621 --> 00:11:19,148
sides with no one disputes with no one.

108
00:11:19,168 --> 00:11:24,216
He employs the speech currently used in the world without adhering to it.

109
00:11:24,538 --> 00:11:31,831
So when the Buddha says things like, um, action, I, I, uh, I call the will action.

110
00:11:32,351 --> 00:11:37,139
I, this, this, I is simply a verbal,

111
00:11:37,322 --> 00:11:47,033
something that is currently used to address, to express something from myself, a self-referential term.

112
00:11:47,474 --> 00:11:55,562
It doesn't mean that the Buddha therefore believes in a self or a real person or a person that is substantial.

113
00:11:56,203 --> 00:11:58,166
It's just the convention of language.

114
00:12:00,849 --> 00:12:05,793
Now here Sariputta is standing by the Buddha fanning him.

115
00:12:06,549 --> 00:12:12,538
And he thought to himself, the Blessed One indeed speaks of abandoning these things through direct experience.

116
00:12:13,299 --> 00:12:18,648
The Sublime One indeed speaks of relinquishing of these things through direct knowledge.

117
00:12:19,970 --> 00:12:28,582
As the Venerable Sariputta considered this, because his mind stopped clinging, because he stopped clinging, he was liberated from the taints.

118
00:12:29,384 --> 00:12:35,712
So remember the taints are the desire, the desire to become, and ignorance, sometimes wrong view.

119
00:12:36,283 --> 00:12:44,472
And so right here now, Sariputta listening to the Buddha becomes an Arahant, completely drops all the taints.

120
00:12:47,797 --> 00:12:53,823
And the wanderer, Dīgha-nākā, for him the spotless immaculate vision of the Dharma arose.

121
00:12:53,884 --> 00:12:57,427
In other words, he became a stream-enterer, the first attainment.

122
00:12:58,149 --> 00:13:01,633
All that is subject to arising is subject to cessation.

123
00:13:02,355 --> 00:13:10,043
And the wanderer Diganaka saw the Dhamma, attained the Dhamma, the truth, attained, understood the Dhamma, fathomed the Dhamma.

124
00:13:10,524 --> 00:13:12,586
He crossed beyond doubt.

125
00:13:13,426 --> 00:13:21,594
So somebody who has that first path improved can no longer doubt the Buddha's teachings because it's his personal experience.

126
00:13:23,375 --> 00:13:31,923
Done away with perplexity, he is resolute and courageous and become independent of others in the teacher's dispensation.

127
00:13:32,950 --> 00:13:40,903
And, of course, the discourse ends with the usual pion, magnificent Master Gautama, magnificent.

128
00:13:42,485 --> 00:13:54,225
Master Gautama has made the Dharma clear in many ways, as though he were turning upright what had been overthrown, revealing what was hidden, showing the way to one who was lost, holding up a lamp in the dark to those who can see.

129
00:13:54,245 --> 00:13:59,092
I go to Master Gautama for refuge, to the Dharma and to the Sangha,

130
00:13:59,511 --> 00:14:06,922
From today, let Master Gautama remember me as a lay follower who has gone to him for refuge, for life.

131
00:14:07,903 --> 00:14:12,009
So Dighanaka becomes one of his followers.

132
00:14:12,490 --> 00:14:19,899
So the teaching here is, you know, let go of views and opinions or at least see them as just

133
00:14:20,268 --> 00:14:31,261
perspective, not to hold on to them and to realize that the process of liberation is nothing to do with thinking about it, nothing to do with holding these views.

134
00:14:31,503 --> 00:14:33,445
It doesn't matter how many books you read.

135
00:14:35,206 --> 00:14:38,672
If they're inspirational, then they have a purpose.

136
00:14:38,991 --> 00:14:48,705
But the whole thing is to come back to the vipassana, come back to constantly asking ourselves these questions about impermanence, not self, and

137
00:14:48,989 --> 00:14:53,278
And in daily life, mainly how we're creating problems for ourselves.

138
00:14:53,960 --> 00:14:59,451
So this distinction between sitting and daily life, you know, try to close that gap.

139
00:15:00,114 --> 00:15:05,765
And by being mindful with everything we do, we can see especially this desire, you know.

140
00:15:06,352 --> 00:15:13,461
And then you're holding on to things and simple things like noticing that time is gone.

141
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:19,586
You know, a meal has gone, one less lunch in this lifetime, things like that.

142
00:15:20,067 --> 00:15:27,716
So you bring that sort of mentality into your daily life and that's what will lead us on.

143
00:15:28,037 --> 00:15:28,397
Okay.

144
00:15:29,798 --> 00:15:33,863
So I can only hope this discourse has been inspirational.

145
00:15:34,432 --> 00:15:48,100
and that you will, by your courageous and unfailing efforts, become like Dīgha Nāga, stream-entrant, and even like Sariputta, fully liberated, sooner rather than later.

146
00:15:48,120 --> 00:15:49,201
So we can do some sitting.

147
00:16:09,854 --> 00:16:21,923
Haudenosaunee dhamankujeni

